            <div xml:id="d172-a3">

                <pb facs="0383" n="283" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg283"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 705.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Scruggs to Mr. Seward.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chin Kiang</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-03-24"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 24,
                        1880</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 21.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I had the honor to receive, on the 21st
                    instant, your dispatch No. 63, of the 27th February last. In response thereto, I
                    regret to say there is not a school of any kind, native or foreign public or
                    private, secular or religious, within this district, in which Chinese are
                    educated by foreign methods or in foreign knowledge. The missionary schools are
                    all conducted in the native language, and their curriculum confined to purely
                    religious and sectarian instruction. A few young men among the native residents
                    of this port take lessons in the English language from a native interpreter
                    educated at Hong-Kong, but now employed herein the customs service. But they
                    seek to know no more of our language than is barely necessary to aid them in
                    business tranactions with foreigners; and what they do thus acquire is little
                    else than the barbarous and childish dialect known as “Pigeon English.” I know
                    of but one exception, and that is the case of General Wong, the military
                    commandant here, an educated Chinaman, who is ambitious to enter the diplomatic
                    service of his country.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM L. SCRUGGS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
